          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-a7a1b4e98b66\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite's “je t'aime” is principally a direct avowal presenting her love for Armand as true. Its repetition and elaboration (“comme je n'aurais pas cru pouvoir aimer”) strongly reinforce T. The surrounding declarations about their future and her abandonment of her former life give the avowal some relationally undertaking force, but much of that commitment is expressed by the following sentences rather than by “je t'aime” alone. “Oh!”, murmuring, repetition, and the embrace indicate emotional intensity but do not independently establish reflex-like expressive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is the degree of P: the utterance participates in a commitment-laden declaration of a shared future, although the explicit undertakings occur chiefly after the core words.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A spoken utterance by Marguerite to Armand, represented as direct dialogue within Armand's first-person narration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite emphatically affirms and elaborates her love while embracing Armand. She immediately projects a happy, tranquil life with him, promises a permanent break with a life of which she is now ashamed, and seeks reassurance that he will not reproach her past. This makes the avowal emotionally intense and embeds it in a prospective relational commitment, without showing that the words themselves are an involuntary or reflex-like discharge.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Oh! oui, je t'aime, mon Armand!”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person avowal strongly supports T; the affirmative framing and direct address identify Armand as its addressee.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je t'aime comme je n'aurais pas cru pouvoir aimer”\",\"supports\":\"The repeated and comparative elaboration presents an unusually strong loving state as true, reinforcing T rather than independently establishing E.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“murmura-t-elle en enlaçant ses deux bras autour de mon cou”\",\"supports\":\"The murmured delivery and embrace establish affectionate intensity and intimacy, but supply no clear evidence that producing the words is reflex-like.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Nous serons heureux, nous vivrons tranquilles, et je dirai un éternel adieu à cette vie”\",\"supports\":\"The projected shared future and promised change of life provide moderate contextual support for performative or undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jamais tu ne me reprocheras le passé, n'est-ce pas?”\",\"supports\":\"Her request for reassurance connects the avowal to expectations governing their relationship, while also showing that the commitment is negotiated in the surrounding speech.\",\"confidence\":0.91},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is located in Chapter XVII of Alexandre Dumas fils's La Dame aux camélias, at source offsets 234484–234493.\",\"supports\":\"Identifies the classified occurrence and its supplied textual location.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, while P captures its limited participation in a commitment-laden relational exchange. Emotional delivery can remain contextual because the passage gives no independent evidence for E, and no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
